{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793004631,"nonce":"MNUvt4dkgn53w/Lcao7RB6392fA=","title":"Clip long editor text runs to the horizontal viewport","message":"Browser CodeEditor uses pooled visible rows plus three overscan rows on each side. Row count is bounded and larger line count alone does not explain the heavier tabs. Horizontal culling excludes wholly offscreen tokens, but a token intersecting the viewport is submitted in full; plain lines bypass that culling entirely. Full-line native caret geometry is retained separately with a 96-line bound.\n\nAn isolated real RayDE browser run compares 200 and 12000 short Haxe lines, a real EditorPart source, and 1000 long comment/plain lines. Ordinary source draws in about 44-46 ms/frame in this driver; long comment/plain rows reach 400-500 ms/frame with roughly 500 ms input latency. The event queue drops nothing. Evidence: bin/web/scroll-before/result.json and matching screenshots. No LSP or extension workload is active in this isolated case.\n\nClip submitted text to horizontal overscan at measured cluster/caret boundaries, retain the complete source/width/selection geometry, and preserve conservative behavior for non-monotone shaped runs. Test the mounted text bound, horizontal pans and geometry including tabs/Unicode. Re-run the browser workload and native editor regressions. Track any remaining base frame cost separately.\n\nAfter each milestone, rebuild WASM and the native session service, stage the verified web assets and restart the local session at its existing address for user testing.","files":null}]}